# Part 0: Framing

| § | Priority | Source | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0.1 Why NCP-AIO after NCA-AIIO | P1 | `[DOC]` | The domain shift argument below |
| 0.2 What changes in the exam | P1 | `[DOC]` | Definitions → decisions |
| 0.3 Why this article is shaped differently | P1 | — | Short, 2 paragraphs |
| 0.4 Honest prerequisites | P1 | — | The section that saves readers $500 |

### 0.1 — the angle

The domain shift **favours ops people**. NCA-AIIO spent 38% on Essential AI Knowledge (loss functions,
metrics, transformers) — the part that punishes an infra background. NCP-AIO moves the weight onto
scheduling, monitoring, troubleshooting and optimization.

For a Kubernetes/Slurm operator that makes it a *downhill* exam conceptually and an *uphill* exam on
NVIDIA-specific tooling. State that trade plainly: you already own the mental models, you are missing
the product names and the CLI verbs.

### 0.2 — definitions vs decisions

One worked contrast is enough to make the point. NCA asks *"what is a Xid error?"*. NCP asks
*"a node logs Xid 94 during a training job — what do you do with the job and what do you do with
the node?"* — two different answers in one question. Everything in Part 3 is built for that shape.

### 0.3 — why not follow the blueprint

Blueprint order teaches badly: it groups by domain, but failures arrive by symptom. At 3AM nobody
opens the "AI Operations" chapter, they grep for the error string. Article 53 was organized by
blueprint because vocabulary has no natural order; this one has one.

Keep the blueprint mapping honest though — add a short table at the end of Part 0 mapping each
blueprint domain to the sections that cover it, same as 53 did. Readers studying for the exam need
to know nothing is missing.

### 0.4 — prerequisites, bluntly

**Lead with the format, because it changes everything.** NCP-AIO is the only certification in the
NVIDIA infrastructure track with **hands-on lab exercises** — 30 multiple-choice questions *plus*
3 live labs in 120 minutes. NCP-AII and NCP-AIN are multiple choice only. The lab environment is
provisioned automatically when the exam starts, and NVIDIA states candidates must be comfortable on
a Linux CLI driving live Slurm, Kubernetes and BCM clusters.

Consequence for the reader, and it is the honest headline of the whole article:
**you cannot pass this one by reading.** Everything in Part 3 has to be something your hands have done.

Then the rest:

- If you have never operated a multi-node GPU cluster, NCP-AIO is not a memorization slog — it is a
  wall. Do the work first, then the exam.
- You need *access* to: a multi-GPU node, an IB fabric, and ideally BCM. Not ownership — access.
- Article 53 (or equivalent knowledge) is assumed. This article does not re-teach InfiniBand vs RoCE,
  MIG vs vGPU, or what Triton is.
- Slurm and Kubernetes basics are assumed. Link articles 51/52 once they ship.

