# Part 1: The Platform You Now Own

Purpose: the vocabulary of *administration*, before troubleshooting it. Keep this tighter than
Phases 1-8 of article 53 — the reader already did that. Target: 25% of the article.

## 1.1 Base Command Manager (BCM) `[HW]` — **P1**

**Why P1:** nobody has a BCM lab, so nobody blogs about it, so this is where readers fail. It is the
highest-value section in Part 1 and the one with the least competition on Google.

Open with the analogy for the K8s reader: **BCM ≈ Cluster API + a golden-image pipeline + fabric
health monitoring, for bare metal.** Then break it down:

- **Architecture:** head node (CMDaemon, the database, provisioning) vs compute nodes. Head node HA
  as a concept — mention it exists, do not go deep.
- **Provisioning flow**, end to end, because this is the thing that clarifies everything else:
  PXE/DHCP → node-installer → image sync → disk layout → finalize → hand off to the workload manager.
  One diagram here if you can draw it.
- **`cmsh` tour.** It is modal, which is the thing that confuses newcomers. The modes worth naming:
  `device`, `category`, `softwareimage`, `network`, `partition`, `monitoring`, `user`, `jobs`.
  Verbs: `list`, `use`, `show`, `set`, `commit`, `refresh`.
- **`commit` is the whole mental model.** Nothing takes effect until you commit — staged edits, like
  editing a manifest before `kubectl apply`. `refresh` throws them away. Say this early and loudly.
- **Image / category / overlay** — the concept most likely to be tested:

  | Concept | What it is | Analogy |
  |---|---|---|
  | Software image | The OS filesystem nodes boot | Container image |
  | Category | Nodes sharing an image + settings | Node pool + DaemonSet selector |
  | Configuration overlay | Targeted config on top, per category or node | Kustomize patch |

- **Changing an image vs changing a running node.** `imageupdate` is the dangerous verb — it syncs
  the image to a live node. Explain dry-run first, and why you never do it to a node running a job.
- **`cm-wlm-setup`** — how BCM actually stands up Slurm or Kubernetes. Worth knowing it exists and
  that it owns the config afterwards, so hand-editing `slurm.conf` fights the tool.
- **Non-interactive `cmsh`** for scripting: `cmsh -c "device; list"`. Useful, and shows you have
  actually used it.
- **Where BCM stops.** It provisions and monitors; it does not schedule. Draw the boundary.

**Capture while you have access:** a `cmsh` session showing image → category → overlay, the output of
`device status`, and one `imageupdate --dry-run`.

## 1.2 DGX OS, NVSM, Fabric Manager, firmware `[HW]` — **P2**

- **DGX OS** is Ubuntu plus NVIDIA's stack. Say what that stack contains so the reader knows what
  breaks: GPU driver, CUDA, DOCA/MOFED for the NICs, DCGM, NVSM, Fabric Manager.
- **`nvidia-fabricmanager`** — the service people forget. On NVSwitch systems (DGX A100/H100 class)
  GPUs will not form NVLink without it, and the failure looks like a mysterious P2P error. Version
  must match the driver exactly. This is a very testable detail and a real 2AM outage.
- **NVSM:** `nvsm show health`, `nvsm show storage`, `nvsm show fans`, `nvsm dump health`. Scope is
  *system* health — PSU, fans, storage, BMC — not GPU compute health. That is DCGM's job.
- **Two bundles, two purposes.** Table it:

  | Bundle | Scope | When |
  |---|---|---|
  | `nvidia-bug-report.sh` | Driver, GPU, Xid, kernel | GPU/driver failure, RMA evidence |
  | `nvsm dump health` | Whole DGX chassis | Hardware/platform failure, NVIDIA Enterprise Support case |

- **Update ordering** matters and is easy to get wrong: BMC/BIOS → NIC firmware → GPU VBIOS →
  driver → CUDA → container toolkit. Mismatches show up as Xid 119/120 or Fabric Manager refusing
  to start.
- **Persistence mode** and `nvidia-persistenced`: why the driver unloading between jobs causes
  multi-second startup latency and flapping metrics.

## 1.3 Slurm for GPUs, beyond 101 `[LAB]`/`[HW]` — **P1**

Link to articles 51/52 for basics. Do not re-teach `sbatch`. Everything here is GPU-specific.

- **Declaring GPUs:** `gres.conf` and `GresTypes=gpu` in `slurm.conf`. `AutoDetect=nvml` — convenient,
  and gets NUMA/CPU affinity wrong often enough that you should verify it rather than trust it.
- **The isolation mechanism**, which is the answer to half of §3.6: `cgroup.conf` with
  `ConstrainDevices=yes` plus `TaskPlugin=task/cgroup`. Without it, `--gres=gpu:1` is advisory and
  jobs can see every GPU on the node. Show the before/after `nvidia-smi` inside a job.
- **Affinity:** `--gpu-bind=closest`, `nvidia-smi topo -m`, and why binding a GPU to a CPU on the
  wrong NUMA node costs real throughput. This is the section that connects to §3.7.
- **Pyxis + Enroot** — how NVIDIA actually runs containers on Slurm, and a genuine gap for someone
  whose reflex is Docker:
  - `enroot import docker://...` → `.sqsh`, unprivileged, no daemon
  - `srun --container-image=... --container-mounts=...` via Pyxis
  - why rootless matters on a shared cluster (no Docker socket to hand out)
  - the gotcha: Enroot needs the NVIDIA hook configured or the container sees no GPU
- **Health enforcement:** `HealthCheckProgram` plus Prolog/Epilog running a DCGM check, so a bad node
  drains itself instead of eating jobs. Ties directly to §3.5.
- **A real multi-node launcher.** One `sbatch` script: `--nodes`, `--gpus-per-node`, `--ntasks-per-node`,
  Pyxis container, `SLURM_PROCID` → `torchrun` rank mapping, `NCCL_DEBUG=INFO` on. This single block
  is worth the whole section — it is the thing people copy.

## 1.4 Kubernetes for GPUs: GPU Operator internals `[LAB]` — **P1**

- **What it actually installs**, in dependency order — and therefore the debug order:
  NFD → GPU Feature Discovery → driver daemonset → container toolkit → device plugin →
  DCGM + DCGM exporter → MIG manager → validators.
- **Validators are symptoms, never causes.** A failing `cuda-validator` means something below it
  broke. Teach the reader to read *down* the chain, not at the loudest pod.
- **Driver container vs host driver:** pick one. `driver.enabled=false` when the driver is preinstalled
  (as on DGX OS). Running both is a top-three cause of a cluster that will not come up.
- **The plumbing:** RuntimeClass `nvidia`, containerd config patching, and the
  `nvidia.com/gpu.deploy.*` node labels the Operator uses to gate its own components. Knowing those
  labels is how you stop a component on one node without uninstalling anything.
- **Sharing, with the honest trade-off table:**

  | Mode | Isolation | Memory guarantee | Needs |
  |---|---|---|---|
  | Time-slicing | None | No | Any GPU, ConfigMap only |
  | MIG | Hardware | Yes | A100/H100+ |
  | vGPU | Hypervisor | Yes | Licence + virtualization |

  Include the time-slicing ConfigMap and the MIG `single` vs `mixed` strategy difference
  (`nvidia.com/gpu` vs `nvidia.com/mig-1g.10gb` resource names).
- **Where K8s is worse than Slurm for training:** no gang scheduling by default, so a distributed job
  can half-schedule and deadlock. Name Kueue / Volcano / Run:ai as the fix. This is a good honest note
  since most readers arrive from the K8s side assuming it is strictly better.

## 1.5 Run:ai, briefly `[DOC]` — **P3**

NVIDIA owns it, so it is on the blueprint. Cover fair-share quotas, projects/departments, node pools,
GPU fractions, and the workload types (training / interactive / inference). Keep it to half a page and
**say if you have not run it** — same honesty as article 53.

