# Part 3: The Symptom Runbook — **the core of the article**

Target: 40% of the article. Use an identical structure for every entry so it is skimmable:

> **Symptom** → **first three commands** → **decision tree** → **root causes** → **fix** → **what to capture for support**

Write these as war stories where you have one. **Fabricated war stories are worse than none** — if you
have not seen it, present it as a documented failure mode and say so.

| § | Symptom | Priority | Needs |
|---|---|---|---|
| 3.1 | Multi-node job hangs, no error, no progress | P1 | `[HW]` |
| 3.2 | GPU util 100%, throughput low | P1 | `[LAB]` |
| 3.3 | "GPU has fallen off the bus" / Xid 79 | P1 | `[HW]` |
| 3.4 | ECC errors and row remapping | P1 | `[HW]` |
| 3.5 | Node drains itself / one node keeps failing jobs | P2 | `[HW]` |
| 3.6 | Container cannot see the GPU | P1 | `[LAB]` |
| 3.7 | IB bandwidth at half of spec | P2 | `[HW]` |
| 3.8 | MIG configuration will not apply | P3 | `[HW]` |
| 3.9 | Storage is starving the GPUs | P2 | `[LAB]`/`[HW]` |
| 3.10 | Thermal throttling / power capping | P2 | `[HW]` |
| 3.11 | Out of memory — three different kinds | P2 | `[LAB]` |

### 3.1 Multi-node job hangs, no error, no progress
NCCL init deadlock — the highest-frequency multi-node failure. Causes to cover: wrong
`NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME` (picking `docker0` or `lo`), IB PKey mismatch, one node's port down, firewall on
the bootstrap network, mismatched world size, and a rank that died without taking the job down.
Show the hang, then the exact log line that reveals it. End with the 4-step triage order:
ranks all present → transport chosen → pairwise RDMA works → collective works.

### 3.2 GPU util 100% but throughput is low
Back-reference §2.1, then the decision tree: SM active low → tiny kernels / launch-bound /
CPU-bound submission. DRAM active high + SM low → memory bound. Both low → starved (dataloader,
storage, host). This is the section that proves you understand the whole stack, so give it room.

### 3.3 "GPU has fallen off the bus" / Xid 79
Differentiate: dead GPU vs power delivery vs thermal shutdown vs riser/seating vs PCIe error.
**Collect `nvidia-bug-report.sh` before rebooting** — the reboot destroys the evidence and "fixes"
nothing. Cover why the same slot failing twice points at the board, not the GPU.

### 3.4 ECC errors and row remapping
Corrected vs uncorrected. `nvidia-smi -q -d ECC`, volatile vs aggregate counters, and the three
remapping states: `Pending` (needs a reset), `Failure` (RMA), plus the healthy incrementing case.
The operational decision — ignore / drain at window / drain now / RMA — as a table. Pair with the
Xid 63-vs-64 and 94-vs-95 distinctions from Part 6.

### 3.5 Node drains itself / jobs keep failing on one node
`sinfo -R` reason strings as the first stop. How Slurm `HealthCheckProgram` and DCGM health watches
interact, and the "up but poisoned" node that passes liveness while failing every job. K8s variant:
node conditions plus the Operator's node-status-exporter.

### 3.6 Container cannot see the GPU
Two variants, both common, one shared triage:
- **Kubernetes:** device plugin not ready, missing RuntimeClass, driver/toolkit version mismatch,
  resource not requested so `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES` is empty, containerd config not patched.
- **Slurm:** gres not requested, cgroup `ConstrainDevices` denying it, Enroot missing the NVIDIA hook.

End with a 5-step triage that works for both: driver on host → runtime configured → device visible in
container → allocation requested → library path correct.

### 3.7 IB bandwidth at half of spec
Link negotiated below rate, job using the NIC that is not nearest its GPU, PCIe link trained at x8
instead of x16, **ACS or IOMMU blocking GPUDirect RDMA**. Evidence: `nvidia-smi topo -m`,
`lspci -vvv` link status, `ibstat` rate. The ACS one is the answer people never guess.

### 3.8 MIG configuration will not apply
Processes still holding the GPU, MIG mode change requiring a GPU reset, unsupported profile
combination, and MIG manager reverting a manual `nvidia-smi mig` change. Short section.

### 3.9 Storage is starving the GPUs
Signature: sawtooth GPU util, high host iowait, low SM active. Causes: too few dataloader workers,
file-per-sample layouts on a parallel FS, no caching, small random reads. Fixes in order of cost:
worker count → sharded formats (webdataset/tar) → local NVMe cache → GPUDirect Storage, with GDS's
actual requirements stated honestly.

### 3.10 Thermal throttling and power capping
`nvidia-smi -q -d PERFORMANCE` clock throttle reasons: SW thermal slowdown, HW thermal slowdown,
SW power cap, HW slowdown. Map each to a different owner — your problem, or the facility's.
Link to article 53 Phase 8 for inlet temp, rack power budget and liquid cooling.

### 3.11 Out of memory — three different OOMs
GPU OOM (CUDA OOM in the traceback) vs host OOM (dmesg oom-killer) vs cgroup/pod OOMKill.
Different signature, different fix, frequently confused. Footnote fragmentation and
`PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF`. Note that a GPU OOM after hours of training is usually a memory leak in
eval, not a sizing mistake.

