# Part 6: Quick Recall — Exam-Day Cheat Sheet

Tables only. If you remember nothing else from this article, remember these.

> **Verify before you trust.** Xid meanings and DCGM field IDs both change between driver and DCGM
> releases. The authoritative sources are the [NVIDIA Xid Errors guide](https://docs.nvidia.com/deploy/xid-errors/)
> and, for field IDs, `dcgmi dmon -l` on your own cluster — it prints every field the installed
> version supports. Treat this sheet as the shortlist, not the spec.

### Xid → Meaning → What You Actually Do

The only classification that matters operationally: **is the GPU still trustworthy?**

| Xid | Meaning | Class | Action |
|---|---|---|---|
| **13** | Graphics Engine Exception | App | Illegal memory access in the kernel. Blame the job, not the node. Repeats across *different* jobs → then suspect HW. |
| **31** | GPU memory page fault | App | Same story as 13 — bad pointer in user code. The single most common Xid on a healthy cluster. |
| **32** | Invalid or corrupted push buffer stream | Driver/PCIe | DMA path. Check PCIe health, driver version, host memory. |
| **43** | GPU stopped processing | App | Job-fatal, node-safe. |
| **45** | Preemptive cleanup after previous error | Consequence | **Never the root cause.** Something killed the context. Look for the Xid *above* it in `dmesg`. |
| **48** | Double Bit ECC Error (DBE) | **Fatal** | Uncorrectable. Drain, reset the GPU, run `dcgmi diag -r 3`. Recurring → RMA. |
| **61 / 62** | Internal micro-controller breakpoint / halt | HW | Pre-Ampere mostly. Reset; if it returns, RMA. |
| **63** | ECC page retirement / row remapping **recorded** | Info | The GPU healed itself. Log it, watch the rate. Not a page. |
| **64** | ECC page retirement / row remapping **failed** | **Fatal** | The GPU could *not* heal itself. Drain and RMA. 63 vs 64 is a favourite distinction — know it. |
| **68** | NVDEC0 exception | HW | Video decode engine. Rare on training clusters. |
| **74** | **NVLink error** | **Fatal** | NVLink/NVSwitch link problem. Check `nvidia-smi nvlink -s`, reseat/cable, fabric manager logs. On DGX this is a service call. |
| **79** | **GPU has fallen off the bus** | **Fatal** | GPU vanished from PCIe. Power, thermal, riser/seating, or dead GPU. Reboot restores it and proves nothing. Collect `nvidia-bug-report.sh` before rebooting. |
| **92** | High single-bit ECC error rate | Warning | Trending toward failure. Schedule the drain, do not panic. |
| **93** | Non-fatal inforom violation | Info | Note and move on. |
| **94** | **Contained** ECC error | Serious | Ampere+. Uncorrectable but blast radius = one process. Job dies, GPU stays usable. Reset at next window. |
| **95** | **Uncontained** ECC error | **Fatal** | Ampere+. Blast radius unknown. Drain the node now, GPU reset required. |
| **109** | Context switch timeout | HW/Driver | Often accompanies other Xids. |
| **119 / 120** | GSP RPC timeout / GSP error | Driver/FW | Hopper+ GSP firmware. Driver and firmware version hunt; `gsp_logs` in the bug report. |

**The 94 vs 95 rule, in one line:** *contained* = kill the job, *uncontained* = kill the node.

**Where to read them:**

```bash
dmesg -T | grep -i xid                  # the source of truth
nvidia-smi -q | grep -i -A2 xid         # per-GPU summary
dcgmi dmon -e 230                       # DCGM_FI_DEV_XID_ERRORS, live
```

### Decision Table: Ignore / Drain / RMA

The judgement call NCP-AIO likes to ask as a scenario.

| Signal | Decision |
|---|---|
| Xid 13/31/43 on one job, other jobs fine | **Ignore the node.** Tell the user their code faulted. |
| Xid 13/31 from many different users on one node | **Drain.** It stopped being an app bug. |
| Corrected (single-bit) ECC, low steady rate | **Ignore**, keep the counter on a dashboard. |
| Xid 92, rising SBE rate | **Drain at next maintenance window.** |
| Xid 63, remapped rows incrementing slowly | **Ignore**, trend it. |
| `Remapped Rows: Pending = Yes` | **Drain** — remap applies after GPU reset. |
| `Remapped Rows: Failure = Yes` | **RMA.** |
| Xid 48 / 95, first occurrence | **Drain**, GPU reset, `dcgmi diag -r 3`. Pass → return to service. |
| Xid 48 / 95 recurring after reset | **RMA.** |
| Xid 64 | **RMA.** |
| Xid 79 | **Drain + physical inspection.** Recurring on the same slot → RMA GPU, then suspect the riser/board. |
| Xid 74 | **Drain**, escalate to NVIDIA. Do not chase it yourself on DGX. |
| `dcgmi diag -r 3` fails on any subtest | **Drain.** Attach the output to the case. |

### Symptom → First Command

| Symptom | Start here |
|---|---|
| Multi-node job hangs at startup | `NCCL_DEBUG=INFO` — read the transport it chose |
| Training slower than expected, no errors | `dcgmi dmon -e 1002,1005` — SM active vs DRAM active |
| GPU util 100%, throughput low | Same as above. Util is not busy-ness. |
| Job cannot see any GPU | `nvidia-smi` inside the container, then `echo $CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES` |
| Node keeps failing jobs | `sinfo -R` (Slurm) / `kubectl describe node` (K8s) |
| Suspect bad GPU | `dcgmi diag -r 3` (exclusive — drain first) |
| Suspect the fabric | `ibstat` on both ends, then `ibdiagnet` |
| Bandwidth below spec | `nvidia-smi topo -m` — is the job using the NIC nearest its GPU? |
| GPU clocks low | `nvidia-smi -q -d PERFORMANCE` — read the throttle reasons |
| Something is on fire, support is asking | `nvidia-bug-report.sh` and `nvsm dump health` |

### Tool → Job

| Tool | What it is for | Not for |
|---|---|---|
| `nvidia-smi` | Quick state: driver, memory, temp, clocks, processes | Performance analysis — its util metric misleads |
| `dcgmi` | Fleet monitoring, profiling metrics, active diagnostics | One-off eyeballing |
| **DCGM Exporter** | DCGM → Prometheus, with pod/job labels | Anything needing sub-second resolution |
| `nvsm` | DGX-system-level health (fans, PSU, storage, BMC) | Non-DGX hardware |
| `nvidia-bug-report.sh` | The bundle NVIDIA support asks for | Reading yourself, mostly |
| `ibstat` / `ibstatus` | Is *this* port up, and at what rate | Fabric-wide problems |
| `ibdiagnet` | Fabric-wide sweep: topology, errors, bad links | Per-job debugging |
| `perfquery` | Raw IB error counters (cumulative — clear first) | Interpreting without a baseline |
| `ib_write_bw` / `ib_read_lat` | Pairwise RDMA baseline, no CUDA involved | Collective performance |
| **nccl-tests** | Collective bandwidth as the app sees it | Isolating *which* link is bad |
| `nsys` (Nsight Systems) | Timeline: where the step actually spends time | Per-kernel instruction analysis (that is Nsight Compute) |
| `cmsh` | BCM cluster administration | Job scheduling |

### DCGM Field IDs Worth Knowing

| ID | Field | Why |
|---|---|---|
| 203 | `DEV_GPU_UTIL` | The metric that lies. Know it to distrust it. |
| **1001** | `PROF_GR_ENGINE_ACTIVE` | Graphics/compute engine active — closest honest analogue to "busy" |
| **1002** | `PROF_SM_ACTIVE` | Fraction of SMs with work resident. **The real utilization number.** |
| 1003 | `PROF_SM_OCCUPANCY` | Warps resident vs maximum — are the SMs *full* |
| **1004** | `PROF_PIPE_TENSOR_ACTIVE` | Tensor Core activity. Low on a training job = you are not using the hardware you paid for. |
| **1005** | `PROF_DRAM_ACTIVE` | Memory bandwidth. High with low SM active = memory bound. |
| 1009 / 1010 | `PROF_PCIE_TX/RX_BYTES` | Host↔GPU traffic — dataloader pressure |
| 1011 / 1012 | `PROF_NVLINK_TX/RX_BYTES` | Intra-node collective traffic |
| 150 | `DEV_GPU_TEMP` | Thermal |
| 155 | `DEV_POWER_USAGE` | Power capping correlation |
| 230 | `DEV_XID_ERRORS` | Xid as a metric — alert on this |
| 310 / 311 | `DEV_ECC_CURRENT` / `_PENDING` | ECC mode, and whether a reboot is owed |
| 312 / 313 | `DEV_ECC_SBE_VOL_TOTAL` / `_DBE_VOL_TOTAL` | Volatile ECC counts (reset on reboot) |
| 314 / 315 | `DEV_ECC_SBE_AGG_TOTAL` / `_DBE_AGG_TOTAL` | Aggregate ECC counts (lifetime) — the RMA evidence |

**The triage triple:** `1002` (SM active), `1005` (DRAM active), `1004` (Tensor active). Those three
locate almost every performance problem before you open a profiler.

| 1002 | 1005 | Verdict |
|---|---|---|
| Low | Low | Starved — dataloader, storage, or host CPU |
| Low | High | Memory bandwidth bound |
| High | Low | Compute bound — the good case |
| High | High | Well fed; tune precision or parallelism next |

### NCCL Environment Variables

| Variable | Effect | When |
|---|---|---|
| `NCCL_DEBUG=INFO` | Print topology and transport choice | **Always, first.** Confirms IB vs TCP. |
| `NCCL_DEBUG_SUBSYS=INIT,GRAPH,ENV` | Narrow the firehose | Hangs during init |
| `NCCL_IB_HCA=mlx5_0,mlx5_1` | Restrict to specific HCAs | Multi-NIC nodes picking the wrong one |
| `NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME=ib0` (or `^docker0,lo`) | Bootstrap interface | Init hangs — usually the actual fix |
| `NCCL_IB_DISABLE=1` | Force TCP | **Diagnostic only.** Works but slow = fabric problem confirmed. |
| `NCCL_P2P_DISABLE=1` | Disable NVLink/PCIe P2P | Diagnostic; suspected NVLink fault |
| `NCCL_ALGO` / `NCCL_PROTO` | Force ring/tree, LL/LL128/Simple | Last resort, with a measurement |
| `NCCL_IB_GID_INDEX` | RoCE GID selection | RoCE only, and it is always this when RoCE breaks |

> **Rule:** every one of these left in a production launch script needs a comment saying which
> incident put it there. Undocumented NCCL env vars are how a cluster silently loses half its bandwidth.

### `cmsh` → `kubectl`

BCM is the piece with no cloud-native muscle memory, so map it explicitly. The big difference:
**`cmsh` is transactional — nothing takes effect until `commit`.**

| `cmsh` | Rough `kubectl` equivalent |
|---|---|
| `device; list` | `kubectl get nodes` |
| `device; use node001; show` | `kubectl describe node node001` |
| `device; status` | `kubectl get nodes -o wide` |
| `category; list` | node pools / label groups |
| `softwareimage; list` | `docker images`, for bare metal |
| `set <property> <value>` | editing a manifest |
| **`commit`** | **`kubectl apply`** |
| `refresh` | discard staged edits |
| `imageupdate -n node001` | rollout to a running node — **the dangerous one** |
| `device; power on/off/reset -n node001` | no equivalent; this is real BMC power control |
| `main; status` | `kubectl get componentstatuses` |

**Image vs category vs overlay**, the thing worth having straight before exam day:

| BCM concept | What it is | Analogy |
|---|---|---|
| Software image | The OS filesystem nodes boot | Container image |
| Category | A group of nodes sharing an image + settings | Node pool + DaemonSet selector |
| Configuration overlay | Targeted config on top, by category or node | Kustomize patch |

### Networking One-Liners

| Question | Answer |
|---|---|
| Link up but slow? | `ibstat` — compare *negotiated* rate against the port's spec |
| Which NIC for which GPU? | `nvidia-smi topo -m` — pick the one sharing a PCIe switch |
| GPUDirect RDMA not working? | ACS and IOMMU. Disable ACS on the switch upstream. |
| Counters look terrible? | They are cumulative. Clear, reproduce, re-read. |
| Who manages the subnet? | OpenSM (free, CLI) or UFM (licensed, telemetry + GUI + alerting) |
| RoCE vs InfiniBand debug | RoCE adds PFC/ECN and GID selection — twice the failure modes |

