# Appendix A: A home lab that is actually useful `[LAB]` — **P1**

**Promoted to P1** once the format was confirmed: with 3 hands-on labs on the exam, a practice
environment stopped being a nice-to-have and became the study plan. Reframe the section accordingly —
this is not "extra credit for enthusiasts", it is where the reader earns the lab points.

Written for the reader with one GPU and no budget. Be precise about the ceiling.

**What one consumer GPU genuinely teaches:**

| Skill | How |
|---|---|
| GPU Operator end to end | k3s/minikube + Operator, then break each component deliberately |
| DCGM + exporter + Grafana | Full stack, including the pod label join |
| The util-vs-SM-active lesson (§2.1) | A loop kernel and two panels |
| Container runtime debugging (§3.6) | Remove the toolkit, remove the RuntimeClass, watch what breaks |
| Time-slicing | ConfigMap, then oversubscribe and watch isolation not happen |
| Nsight Systems timelines | Any small training script |
| Dataloader starvation (§3.9) | Throttle workers to 0, watch the sawtooth |
| Slurm single-node + Pyxis/Enroot | Full install, container jobs, cgroup isolation |
| GPU OOM vs host OOM (§3.11) | Trivially reproducible |

**What it cannot teach, and say so:** MIG (needs A100/H100+), NVLink/NVSwitch and Fabric Manager,
any InfiniBand, BCM, vGPU, real ECC/Xid hardware failures, multi-node NCCL at speed.

**The cheapest approximation of multi-node:** two machines with Ethernet + NCCL over sockets. It
teaches rank plumbing, `NCCL_SOCKET_IFNAME`, gang-scheduling deadlocks and launcher scripts — all of
§3.1 except the fabric itself. Worth doing.

